00:07:58.315 --> 00:08:03.101
Now, what I think is going to
determine which way Nigeria goes is

00:08:03.101 --> 00:08:06.571
really going to be
the quality of leadership.

00:08:06.571 --> 00:08:09.901
And it's going to be
the quality of leadership,

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:12.988
not only at the business level,
but almost,

00:08:12.988 --> 00:08:17.800
I would say, in some respects more
important at the political level.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.903
Because I think that the political
level determines the framework which

00:08:22.903 --> 00:08:28.855
allows business leadership either to excel
or it constrains business leadership.

00:08:28.855 --> 00:08:33.087
And just to make an international
comparison, if we just look at two

00:08:33.087 --> 00:08:37.782
countries in the early 60s, and
I'll use the Middle East as an example.

00:08:37.782 --> 00:08:42.030
You look at the United Arab Emirates and
you look at Libya.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:47.397
Both had 1 to 1.5 million
barrels a day of oil.

00:08:47.397 --> 00:08:52.125
Both had populations that were not
largely educated in the early 60s.

00:08:52.125 --> 00:08:56.611
In many respects, Libya was ahead
because it was in the Mediterranean,

00:08:56.611 --> 00:09:01.920
and you'd have thought that with
proximity to Europe assisted it greatly.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.276
But now when you look at it 50
years later, why has Abu Dhabi,

00:09:07.276 --> 00:09:12.837
why has Dubai taken off from desert
towns that nobody thought were

00:09:12.837 --> 00:09:19.735
going to be catapulted to where they are
right now, and where is Libya right now?

00:09:19.735 --> 00:09:23.472
That really came down to
the quality of leadership.

00:09:23.472 --> 00:09:24.670
&gt;&gt; So let's talk about that.

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:28.761
So good governance is a precondition for
broad based, robust,

00:09:28.761 --> 00:09:33.020
persistent economic growth,
let's put it that way.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.586
So what is the impetus, because we
all know why there's bad governance,

00:09:36.586 --> 00:09:39.706
because there are certain interests
that are being protected.

00:09:39.706 --> 00:09:43.653
And in a lot of economies, I've worked
in many, many countries in my life,

00:09:43.653 --> 00:09:48.037
it's usually that very powerful vested
legacy interests are doing everything they

00:09:48.037 --> 00:09:52.258
can to maintain their place and to prevent
new entrants to prevent competition.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:56.768
And they do that through all sorts
of import duties, export licenses,

00:09:56.768 --> 00:10:00.844
quotas, regulation that's
designed to reduce competition.

00:10:00.844 --> 00:10:03.199
Where will the impetus for
good governance come?

00:10:03.199 --> 00:10:07.982
Will it come from countries realizing
that they're all competing for

00:10:07.982 --> 00:10:10.751
the same human and investment capital?

00:10:10.751 --> 00:10:15.449
And that the winners will be the ones
that create a more stable, reliable,

00:10:15.449 --> 00:10:17.323
predictable playing field?

00:10:17.323 --> 00:10:19.305
&gt;&gt; I think it's going to come in two ways.

00:10:19.305 --> 00:10:25.883
On the one hand, the crisis or
the difficulties the countries endure,

00:10:25.883 --> 00:10:31.154
in many respects triggers
off a certain introspection.

00:10:31.154 --> 00:10:34.916
And I'll just say that if you
look at countries like Japan,

00:10:34.916 --> 00:10:37.183
the crisis of the Second World War.

00:10:37.183 --> 00:10:42.111
Look at what happened to Europe, and
you can see the way in which it ushered in

00:10:42.111 --> 00:10:45.966
years of enormous growth in the 1950s and
early 60s.

00:10:45.966 --> 00:10:48.725
So I think crisis forces people and

00:10:48.725 --> 00:10:53.588
forces nations to think again,
and to rethink their model.

00:10:53.588 --> 00:10:56.061
So I think that that's
going to be one impetus.

00:10:56.061 --> 00:11:00.398
And therefore I think that paradoxically,
the COVID situation, the energy

00:11:00.398 --> 00:11:06.140
transition, is going to lead to a certain
amount of rethinking, that's one aspect.

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:10.372
The second thing which I'd like to point
out, which is usually not often pointed

00:11:10.372 --> 00:11:13.686
out, it comes from what you've
said about vested interests.

00:11:13.686 --> 00:11:17.412
I'd like to take a slightly
different angle on what you've said.

00:11:17.412 --> 00:11:20.898
I think that there will always
be vested interests and

00:11:20.898 --> 00:11:25.469
there will always be powerful groups
who take care of themselves, and

00:11:25.469 --> 00:11:28.581
I'm not so
sure that's the crucial question.

00:11:28.581 --> 00:11:35.935
I think the crucial question is, that
elite that is taking care of themselves,

00:11:35.935 --> 00:11:42.766
what is their long-term vision for
themselves and for their countries?

00:11:42.766 --> 00:11:48.999
Do they have a long-term vision in
which they still want to be on top but

00:11:48.999 --> 00:11:53.649
they have the sense that they
have got to do enough for

00:11:53.649 --> 00:11:57.576
the base of the population
to remain there?

00:11:57.576 --> 00:12:04.393
And do they have a picture of themselves
as being the leaders of great countries?

00:12:04.393 --> 00:12:09.642
And you can characterize several countries
who are very much like that, all right?

00:12:09.642 --> 00:12:15.026
Or is their vision very extractive?

00:12:15.026 --> 00:12:17.920
They don't really care what
happens to everybody else.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.010
&gt;&gt; They have three passports,
their money's in a bank somewhere else.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:24.566
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and so deep down
they don't really care, all right?

00:12:24.566 --> 00:12:27.975
And they don't have a sense of patriotism.

00:12:27.975 --> 00:12:31.560
And I see it very much in, for example,
I'll go back to the Middle East.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:35.620
And I see very much that however well to
do I see some of the leading families in

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:39.439
the Middle East, it's still important
that Emirates does very well.

00:12:39.439 --> 00:12:41.789
[MUSIC]

00:12:41.789 --> 00:12:45.069
&gt;&gt; Hakeem has plenty of
experience with extractive elites

00:12:45.069 --> 00:12:48.145
from the six years he ran
the United Bank for Africa.

00:12:48.145 --> 00:12:53.003
This time at UBA gave him a deeper
