absolutely talking nonsense, okay?

00:15:41.557 --> 00:15:47.746
Or in some cases they just could not take
change, human beings don't like change.

00:15:47.746 --> 00:15:50.209
And maybe if I was one of them,
I would have reacted the same.

00:15:50.209 --> 00:15:51.984
So that was the pivotal moment for me.

00:15:51.984 --> 00:15:55.120
And it was then to just finally go and
sit down and.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:58.673
Talk to a whole lot of them,
very hostile environment.

00:15:58.673 --> 00:16:02.020
Mr. Belo-Osagie,
why do you hate people so much?

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:03.590
Why are you so mean?

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:05.230
Why are you so vicious?

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:09.166
Why do you like sacking people,
just because you're privileged?

00:16:09.166 --> 00:16:13.176
And explaining that the path
we are taking is going to

00:16:13.176 --> 00:16:16.652
lead us to the complete
collapse of the bank.

00:16:16.652 --> 00:16:18.837
And they say, no,
it's never going to happen.

00:16:18.837 --> 00:16:21.253
Climate change, never going to happen.

00:16:21.253 --> 00:16:26.108
And you're trying to break it down to them
and say, this is where we're going to go.

00:16:26.108 --> 00:16:29.976
All right,
I will go the way you're going, but

00:16:29.976 --> 00:16:33.760
these are the signs that
you're going to see.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:39.950
And as you see those signs,
let's reconsider, all right?

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:43.220
And so
I think that one was a pivotal one for

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:47.241
me because my fear was,
I need to carry them along,

00:16:47.241 --> 00:16:52.215
but will they realize it so
late that we've gone over the edge?

00:16:52.215 --> 00:16:53.544
&gt;&gt; So, that's fascinating.

00:16:53.544 --> 00:16:57.019
So it was your sense that some
meaningful proportion of the bank's

00:16:57.019 --> 00:17:01.440
employees had no idea about its financial
troubles, or didn't fully understand.

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:02.553
&gt;&gt; They didn't fully understand.

00:17:02.553 --> 00:17:04.270
&gt;&gt; Did that include managers,
senior people?

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.131
&gt;&gt; Yeah, managers in particular.

00:17:06.131 --> 00:17:07.269
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]

00:17:07.269 --> 00:17:08.229
&gt;&gt; Did not understand the cash

00:17:08.229 --> 00:17:09.067
position of this bank.

00:17:09.067 --> 00:17:13.044
&gt;&gt; They didn't understand where it was
inevitably going to lead because previous

00:17:13.044 --> 00:17:15.609
to that,
a lot of the accounts had been falsified.

00:17:15.609 --> 00:17:18.729
So they were just declaring these profits,
but

00:17:18.729 --> 00:17:21.855
effectively they were
eroding their capital.

00:17:21.855 --> 00:17:23.817
And most of them were not aware of it.

00:17:23.817 --> 00:17:28.163
For a lot of them, for example,
didn't realize that, look,

00:17:28.163 --> 00:17:31.779
we are banking old customers
who are going nowhere.

00:17:31.779 --> 00:17:34.690
There is a whole new group
of customers that we're

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:39.070
not banking who are going to be the
future, and we need to start banking them.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:43.536
&gt;&gt; Managing a sometimes hostile workforce
taught Hakeem about the empathy

00:17:43.536 --> 00:17:45.816
required to be an effective leader.

00:17:45.816 --> 00:17:48.241
&gt;&gt; So you needed to help them
understand that this was a crisis.

00:17:48.241 --> 00:17:48.770
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:50.253
then you needed to help
them have a vision-

00:17:50.253 --> 00:17:50.800
&gt;&gt; Yes,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:51.646
&gt;&gt; Of a way out?

00:17:51.646 --> 00:17:52.376
&gt;&gt; Yes, correct.

00:17:52.376 --> 00:17:54.572
&gt;&gt; And
you committed to metrics along the way so

00:17:54.572 --> 00:17:57.193
that people could see what
was actually happening?

00:17:57.193 --> 00:17:59.818
&gt;&gt; Yes, now, there's another point though.

00:17:59.818 --> 00:18:06.563
I also needed to understand
why they hated me, all right?

00:18:06.563 --> 00:18:09.685
I didn't understand it until that event,
until this lady,

00:18:09.685 --> 00:18:13.354
I remember she came up to me and
she just said to me, Mr. Belo-Osagie,

00:18:13.354 --> 00:18:16.987
I'm going to give you a chance because
you said something of the speech.

00:18:16.987 --> 00:18:21.955
You said that, you don't like firing
people, and you told a story about

00:18:21.955 --> 00:18:26.612
how you read a story to your daughter
before she went to bed at night.

00:18:26.612 --> 00:18:31.457
because your daughter asks you, dad,
people in school keep saying that

00:18:31.457 --> 00:18:35.963
your dad likes retrenching people,
what does retrench mean?

00:18:35.963 --> 00:18:40.415
So the lady looks at me and said, the fact
that you could actually read to your

00:18:40.415 --> 00:18:43.976
daughter at night means that
you can't be completely evil.

00:18:43.976 --> 00:18:46.318
That's the exact word she used, okay?

00:18:46.318 --> 00:18:49.077
And I think that it suddenly struck me

00:18:49.077 --> 00:18:53.357
that I had to also think about
how I was appearing to them.

00:18:53.357 --> 00:18:58.212
And then I had to realize that a lot
of the people who came in to change

00:18:58.212 --> 00:19:00.395
the bank were people like me.

00:19:00.395 --> 00:19:05.379
And therefore there were not many people
who were like the people who were

00:19:05.379 --> 00:19:07.511
trying to change, all right?

00:19:07.511 --> 00:19:10.677
And therefore there was
a cultural conflict as well.

00:19:10.677 --> 00:19:16.824
And I needed to now start bringing
other kinds of people to work with me,

00:19:16.824 --> 00:19:22.365
who were closer to the people I
was trying to change, all right?

00:19:22.365 --> 00:19:26.569
Because they could better sell
what we were trying to do.

00:19:26.569 --> 00:19:30.854
So, for example, if you're running
a company in which they're all high school

00:19:30.854 --> 00:19:34.194
educated folks, and
that's how banks were in those days, and

00:19:34.194 --> 00:19:37.787
you're arriving with a group of
trendy investment banking types,

00:19:37.787 --> 00:19:40.457
all MBAs,
you're obviously setting yourself.

00:19:40.457 --> 00:19:44.040
[LAUGH] But I didn't see that at the time.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:49.973
But that was a critical event in which
I saw I needed to change them and

00:19:49.973 --> 00:19:53.109
I saw how I needed to change myself.

00:19:53.109 --> 00:20:00.454
[MUSIC]

00:20:00.454 --> 00:20:05.176
&gt;&gt; You've been involved as an investor, as
a mentor, or somehow tangentially involved

00:20:05.176 --> 00:20:08.165
in some of the biggest tech
startups on the continent.

00:20:08.165 --> 00:20:12.401
And I was going to ask you,
there seems to be a lot of frothy

00:20:12.401 --> 00:20:17.284
excitement about health tech,
EdTech, MedTech, FinTech,

00:20:17.284 --> 00:20:21.356
and a lot of new capital
flowing into these sectors.

00:20:21.356 --> 00:20:25.064
Does the market justify this
level of excitement and energy?

00:20:25.064 --> 00:20:28.383
And is there enough for everyone to grow?

00:20:28.383 --> 00:20:31.693
And just in the Nigerian market,
let's go to the continent in a second.

00:20:31.693 --> 00:20:36.181
&gt;&gt; All right, the first thing I'll
say to you is that there's really

00:20:36.181 --> 00:20:38.921
nothing unusual about the excitement.

00:20:38.921 --> 00:20:43.060
There was excitement about the banking
industry in Nigeria at one time, and

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:44.815
we had 100 banks at one time.

00:20:44.815 --> 00:20:49.497
There was an excitement at one point about
the oil and gas industry, and we had

00:20:49.497 --> 00:20:54.266
about 100 groups who were all setting
up their Mickey Mouse oil companies.

00:20:54.266 --> 00:20:58.060
And in a sense, that is probably,
in my view, a good thing.

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:03.474
Because what it does mean is that
rather than a government agency,

00:21:03.474 --> 00:21:08.791
all right, only allow three or
four powerfully connected groups

00:21:08.791 --> 00:21:13.738
to dominate an area,
you have a huge amount of competition.

00:21:13.738 --> 00:21:17.550
And then inevitably,
there's going to be a shakeout.

00:21:17.550 --> 00:21:21.203
Some are going to survive and
be very strong and

00:21:21.203 --> 00:21:25.989
very profitable and
many others will just go by the wayside.

00:21:25.989 --> 00:21:29.961
Now, in the early years,
everybody will be doing well.

00:21:29.961 --> 00:21:33.831
But inevitably what then
happens is that some can no

00:21:33.831 --> 00:21:38.241
longer continue to lose the money
that they're losing and

00:21:38.241 --> 00:21:43.731
the special breaks are no longer there and
they will fall by the wayside.

00:21:43.731 --> 00:21:47.933
And I've seen it, the oil and gas
industry, we saw it in banking in Nigeria,

00:21:47.933 --> 00:21:50.468
and we're going to see
the same thing in tech.

00:21:50.468 --> 00:21:55.981
And out of this, I think a couple
of very strong groups will emerge,

00:21:55.981 --> 00:22:02.286
which have a combination of the basic
model is very good, it's a good model.

00:22:02.286 --> 00:22:07.394
The second element of it is that they
have had to make that adaptation from

00:22:07.394 --> 00:22:12.337
brilliant idea to effective management
that successfully scales and

00:22:12.337 --> 00:22:14.282
handles that transition.

00:22:14.282 --> 00:22:16.558
And not all groups can do that.

00:22:16.558 --> 00:22:22.382
And then at the same time, it's also
got to maintain a good relationship and

00:22:22.382 --> 00:22:26.841
understanding of the national
regulatory environment,

00:22:26.841 --> 00:22:30.942
which in the sense,
forms the basis of competition.

00:22:30.942 --> 00:22:33.770
[MUSIC]

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:38.563
&gt;&gt; That national regulatory environment
may be the biggest key to whether these

00:22:38.563 --> 00:22:42.188
tech startups,
particularly in finance, can succeed.

00:22:42.188 --> 00:22:44.886
&gt;&gt; I think that the FinTech companies,

00:22:44.886 --> 00:22:48.286
they will go much farther
than people expect.

00:22:48.286 --> 00:22:54.152
And that the only thing I think that will
restrain them is going to be regulation.

00:22:54.152 --> 00:22:59.652
It's a regulatory environment
that thinks banks and

00:22:59.652 --> 00:23:05.526
sees the protection of
the existing banks as number one.

00:23:05.526 --> 00:23:09.663
&gt;&gt; I've heard you say in a previous
interview that for a lot of businesses

00:23:09.663 --> 00:23:14.588
how you manage the government is 50 to
60% of your work in a place like Nigeria.

00:23:14.588 --> 00:23:15.921
What does that mean?

00:23:15.921 --> 00:23:20.855
&gt;&gt; That means that because
the government is such a critical

00:23:20.855 --> 00:23:26.314
market player in the sense that
it's either buying services.

00:23:26.314 --> 00:23:32.524
Where it is not buying services, it is
providing services on which you depend.

00:23:32.524 --> 00:23:37.089
It is writing the rules of the game,
all right?

00:23:37.089 --> 00:23:42.409
And it has such an overwhelming
power within the judicial system,

00:23:42.409 --> 00:23:46.209
in which yes,
you can enforce your rights, but

00:23:46.209 --> 00:23:53.110
the time taken to enforce your rights,
you may no longer exist as a company.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:24:00.354
Now, if that mindset is accompanied My
mind set on the pot of the bureaucrats or

00:24:00.354 --> 00:24:05.541
the political officials that we have
the right to exercise this power.

00:24:05.541 --> 00:24:11.874
Then you find yourself having to work
to deal with that entity, all right?

00:24:11.874 --> 00:24:12.720
The government.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:15.443
Now, a lot of young people, of course,

00:24:15.443 --> 00:24:20.640
have grown up in an environment in
which government was a bad thing.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:23.879
The less government the better government,
there are children of the Reaganite and

00:24:23.879 --> 00:24:25.480
the Thatcherite generation.

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:29.405
Now my generation is in that sense
different were from the LB Johnson,

00:24:29.405 --> 00:24:30.930
the Kennedy and Vishnu.

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:34.279
It was a good thing.
What it does mean is that they've got to

00:24:34.279 --> 00:24:38.973
move away from thinking of
the government as a hostile group.

00:24:38.973 --> 00:24:42.380
But a government in some
identity in which you've got

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:44.887
to explain to them what you're doing.

00:24:44.887 --> 00:24:47.286
They've got to understand the technology.

00:24:47.286 --> 00:24:50.090
They've got to understand the ecosystem.

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:54.080
And many of them simply
don't understand it.

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.518
And when government officials
don't understand something,

00:24:56.518 --> 00:24:58.590
their instinctive reaction is to say no.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:25:01.600
&gt;&gt; Right, it's so much easier to say no,
it's much less risk.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.706
I'm absolutely stunned at the number
of startups in this space and

00:25:04.706 --> 00:25:08.600
all then sort of Neo banks and non banks
and all the things that they're providing.

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:12.480
Now I'm going to stipulate that there is a
role for regulation to protect consumers.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:12.994
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:25:12.994 --> 00:25:16.686
&gt;&gt; Put yourself in the shoes of the
Nigerian regulator, I think it's NTIDA,

00:25:16.686 --> 00:25:18.105
I don't know which agency.

00:25:18.105 --> 00:25:23.798
But what are they doing to stay current
and to create a regulatory environment

00:25:23.798 --> 00:25:29.154
that serves the function of regulation
without strangling the market?

00:25:29.154 --> 00:25:31.926
&gt;&gt; What I think they need to do more of,
and

00:25:31.926 --> 00:25:35.887
I won't say that they're
against the tech companies.

00:25:35.887 --> 00:25:39.273
Because I've experienced this
kind of situation when we

00:25:39.273 --> 00:25:42.090
first started a securities
trading company.

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:46.328
We had to spend a lot of time talking
to the banking supervision to say we're

00:25:46.328 --> 00:25:47.012
not a bank.

00:25:47.012 --> 00:25:47.927
&gt;&gt; We are not taking deposits.

00:25:47.927 --> 00:25:49.271
&gt;&gt; We're not taking deposits.

00:25:49.271 --> 00:25:52.033
And then to start encouraging them,

00:25:52.033 --> 00:25:58.092
why don't you go visit other countries
that are say five years ahead of Nigeria,

00:25:58.092 --> 00:26:02.370
so you can get a sense of
what the future is like.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:07.454
And then you can then talk to other
regulators who have experienced this and

00:26:07.454 --> 00:26:12.150
who have seen that it's not as
frightening as you think it is.

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:16.439
And then the next thing is that you've got
to let the regulator's know in a way that

00:26:16.439 --> 00:26:18.470
is not condescending, okay?

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:24.348
That they need to understand the issues
and they've got to hire into the central

00:26:24.348 --> 00:26:30.470
bank, a lot of people who are younger
than they are, who understand that.

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:34.720
I believe that participants in
the marketplace have to share with them.

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:39.190
Look, there's some funny things
that are going on, okay?

00:26:39.190 --> 00:26:40.630
And we admit it.

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:46.310
All right, and these are the steps we will
take to ensure that it won't happen again.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:49.830
So therefore, there's a set amount of
self regulation that needs to take place.

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:53.586
And sometimes you've also
got to call yourself out,

00:26:53.586 --> 00:26:57.540
because that builds a certain
trust with regulator.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:00.092
So he doesn't see it as us and them.

00:27:00.092 --> 00:27:05.131
&gt;&gt; What about if the real force
behind the regulation is not visible?

00:27:05.131 --> 00:27:09.079
That there are very powerful entrenched
interests actually turning the dials.

00:27:09.079 --> 00:27:13.399
And I can think of examples where in
pacer tried to move, for example,

00:27:13.399 --> 00:27:16.490
into South Africa and
the bank saw this mile away.

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:17.049
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:27:17.049 --> 00:27:18.296
&gt;&gt; And
said no way these guys [INAUDIBLE] much.

00:27:18.296 --> 00:27:19.100
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:21.355
&gt;&gt; We'll do what they're
doing when we're ready.

00:27:21.355 --> 00:27:22.750
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; This is high risk.

00:27:22.750 --> 00:27:25.310
Trust us, we'll be there eventually.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:27.150
How much of that sort of?

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:28.898
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; Wizard of Oz behind the curtains

00:27:28.898 --> 00:27:29.614
going on here?

00:27:29.614 --> 00:27:34.043
&gt;&gt; Okay, yes,
now where the incumbent waves

00:27:34.043 --> 00:27:38.851
the national flag they're their strongest.

00:27:38.851 --> 00:27:42.743
So the M PESA one into
South Africa to me is expected,

00:27:42.743 --> 00:27:47.531
the existing banks will wave
the national flag, wrap it round.

00:27:47.531 --> 00:27:51.608
We're protecting our nation against these
foreigners who with these bizarre ideas.

00:27:51.608 --> 00:27:59.035
Now, what will be interesting is how
they react to the South African M PESA.

00:27:59.035 --> 00:28:03.845
All right, or a group of younger
techies inspired by an M PESA.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:07.620
Maybe even funded by MPESA.

00:28:07.620 --> 00:28:11.526
And that then poses the question or
says, look,

00:28:11.526 --> 00:28:17.440
you the large incumbents who
grew up in the apartheid regime.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:24.180
What have you done for
lending for the poor folks?

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:29.420
How have you been inclusive and
we think we are taking a shot at that.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:35.060
And why should we be regulated
out of that competition?

00:28:35.060 --> 00:28:38.480
Now that to me would be
an interesting challenge.

00:28:38.480 --> 00:28:41.080
And that to me is some that may happen.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.914
So I think that even within
incurs like Nigeria,

00:28:44.914 --> 00:28:50.336
I think the biggest issue that a lot
of the public has about big banks,

00:28:50.336 --> 00:28:55.200
is that it hasn't in a sense
benefited the wider population,

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:58.031
in the way in which Telecom's has.

00:28:58.031 --> 00:29:04.218
So the Telecom's companies, it started
off, only the well to do headphones.

00:29:04.218 --> 00:29:08.674
All right, but
today the poor have phones and

00:29:08.674 --> 00:29:11.846
pricing has steadily dropped.

00:29:11.846 --> 00:29:18.546
Not that we haven't had that same
inclusive aspect to banking.

00:29:18.546 --> 00:29:27.448
[MUSIC]

00:29:27.448 --> 00:29:30.872
&gt;&gt; Given Hakeem vantage point
on African finance, I wanted his

00:29:30.872 --> 00:29:35.500
opinion on some of the questions that we
explored on Season One of Grit and Growth,

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:39.211
about FinTech and about the future
of debt for small businesses.

00:29:39.211 --> 00:29:44.324
&gt;&gt; What will it take to make credit
affordable to SMEs that want

00:29:44.324 --> 00:29:49.253
$100,000 capex loan in
the African continent?

00:29:49.253 --> 00:29:53.658
Because if I have to say we have
1000 alumni from our program.

00:29:53.658 --> 00:29:57.759
And these are typically, I mean about 30%
of them are tech or tech enabled, but

00:29:57.759 --> 00:30:01.097
most of them are not on some kind of
hockey stick growth trajectory.

00:30:01.097 --> 00:30:02.852
Or if equity was available to them,

00:30:02.852 --> 00:30:06.604
they're actually not interested in
giving up ownership of their company.

00:30:06.604 --> 00:30:13.690
What they want is reasonable, affordable
credit to grow and build their business.

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:16.950
And I don't care what country
you're in with a few exceptions.

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:19.360
The complaint is that it's just not there.

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:22.490
Banks are just not interested in business.

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:25.290
&gt;&gt; Let's look at it from the point of
views as to why don't the banks want

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:26.960
to go into that area?

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:29.880
I think that it comes down
to the following issues.

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:34.674
One is there's got to be enough data
that can predict who repays and

00:30:34.674 --> 00:30:36.480
who doesn't repay.

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:40.430
So I think that the question of
what data is predictive, and

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:42.254
how can you accumulate it?

00:30:42.254 --> 00:30:45.609
I think that's one,
in a very big important aspect.

00:30:45.609 --> 00:30:49.914
Then I think the second
important aspect is what is

00:30:49.914 --> 00:30:55.245
the technology that can allow
us to access these customers and

00:30:55.245 --> 00:31:02.700
utilize this predictive data, in a manner
that drops the cost of doing so.

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:08.050
Because ultimately, if the cost
of acquiring a customer going for

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:12.923
the transaction and
making enough successful repayments,

00:31:12.923 --> 00:31:19.480
that cost has to be less than the revenue
generated from giving the loan.

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:25.870
And ultimately, I think that's a question
of technology, data and how it is done.

00:31:25.870 --> 00:31:30.175
And I think that as you begin to
crack that question, that problem,

00:31:30.175 --> 00:31:33.160
you will then begin to
make progress there.

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:37.931
But I don't think that can be done in
the traditional manner in which we have

00:31:37.931 --> 00:31:40.410
sought to do it till now.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:44.173
&gt;&gt; Although Hakeem sees entrepreneurs
bringing real progress,

00:31:44.173 --> 00:31:47.690
he also preaches patience
&gt;&gt; The thing I want

00:31:47.690 --> 00:31:51.727
to point out is that as
always in lots of new ideas,

00:31:51.727 --> 00:31:57.798
you will always have those companies
who they have the basic idea.

00:31:57.798 --> 00:31:59.786
And they can speak to it.

00:31:59.786 --> 00:32:04.223
And then you've got another set,
which is usually a subset,

00:32:04.223 --> 00:32:08.676
who approach this thing with intense and
unrelenting rigor.

00:32:08.676 --> 00:32:09.683
&gt;&gt; You mean on execution?

00:32:09.683 --> 00:32:10.889
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; In operation?

00:32:10.889 --> 00:32:13.903
&gt;&gt; Yes, and there's always sometimes,

00:32:13.903 --> 00:32:17.839
in some companies,
a desire to move very quickly.

00:32:17.839 --> 00:32:20.999
because you have this sense
that you've got to move quickly

00:32:20.999 --> 00:32:25.403
before your competitors and there's
money flowing in, investing in you.

00:32:25.403 --> 00:32:29.741
And there's the danger
that you then launch and

00:32:29.741 --> 00:32:36.150
you then grow before you have all of
these issues totally figured out.

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:39.546
&gt;&gt; Is that pressure coming from
the investors or from the founders?

00:32:39.546 --> 00:32:41.357
&gt;&gt; In some cases, the investors.

00:32:41.357 --> 00:32:47.116
And I think that part of that is
due to the fact that we still have,

00:32:47.116 --> 00:32:54.384
and I would call it a US-imposed three
to five year cycle in investments, okay?

00:32:54.384 --> 00:32:58.914
And I say US-imposed not because the US
government directed it, but because

00:32:58.914 --> 00:33:03.529
the timing in the United States for a lot
of these things is three to five years.

00:33:03.529 --> 00:33:06.469
And then you-
&gt;&gt; Strategic exit or something.

00:33:06.469 --> 00:33:07.830
&gt;&gt; Exactly.
&gt;&gt; You get your money back out.

00:33:07.830 --> 00:33:14.233
&gt;&gt; Exactly, now,
in my own experience as an entrepreneur,

00:33:14.233 --> 00:33:19.734
three to five years,
I was still turning around.

00:33:19.734 --> 00:33:22.869
I'd realized that I needed
to pivot a little bit here.

00:33:22.869 --> 00:33:26.337
I realized that I'd got some hires wrong,
okay?

00:33:26.337 --> 00:33:32.967
And I found it was really somewhere
between years five and seven, all right?

00:33:32.967 --> 00:33:37.789
Or six, seven, eight,
that I now felt I was in my stride.

00:33:37.789 --> 00:33:41.654
And then you started to get the truly
phenomenal or exponential growth.

00:33:41.654 --> 00:33:44.465
And it had a solid base, all right?

00:33:44.465 --> 00:33:48.656
So I do think that we need
a longer time horizon.

00:33:48.656 --> 00:33:52.120
So there's got to be more
patience in the capital.

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:57.134
And also the founders
themselves needs to acknowledge

00:33:57.134 --> 00:34:03.675
that they've got to do very diligent
work before they roll things out.

00:34:03.675 --> 00:34:08.242
On the one hand, you've got that vision,
and I hate to use that word, but

00:34:08.242 --> 00:34:09.579
you know what I mean.

00:34:09.579 --> 00:34:14.857
But you've got to reduce that to
the series of steps that you need

00:34:14.857 --> 00:34:19.957
to move from where you are to
where hopefully you want to go to.

00:34:19.957 --> 00:34:22.482
And if I have a worry it's that,

00:34:22.482 --> 00:34:27.254
I know lots of young folks who
they've got that big picture,

00:34:27.254 --> 00:34:31.839
but the boring bit of this step and
this step and this step,

00:34:31.839 --> 00:34:37.574
which is often mundane, tiring, and
exhausting, that is as crucial.

00:34:37.574 --> 00:34:40.167
&gt;&gt; And as a founder,
you have to be more than just diligent.

00:34:40.167 --> 00:34:43.483
You've gotta be able to trust yourself and
your process.

00:34:43.483 --> 00:34:46.790
&gt;&gt; Courage is vital in
whatever you're doing.

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:51.988
You're really leading people down
a path in which you pretend you

00:34:51.988 --> 00:34:57.103
can see the end of the tunnel as a leader,
but you can't really.

00:34:57.103 --> 00:35:02.221
But you have a sense that this
is roughly where you need to go.

00:35:02.221 --> 00:35:05.334
I often quote McNamara,
who talked about the fog of war.

00:35:05.334 --> 00:35:10.648
And I deeply believe that there
is also a fog in business.

00:35:10.648 --> 00:35:13.720
There is so much that you don't know.

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:20.528
And I think great leaders have the ability
to live with not knowing all the answers,

00:35:20.528 --> 00:35:26.964
but being able to carry people along and
solve those issues as they come along.

00:35:26.964 --> 00:35:30.628
And having the flexibility
to make those changes.

00:35:30.628 --> 00:35:34.966
And having the sense of security
of saying to themselves,

00:35:34.966 --> 00:35:39.670
don't know everything, but
I'll solve it as it comes along.

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:45.088
The founder has got to have
an appreciation of the complete array

00:35:45.088 --> 00:35:50.320
of talents that he or she needs and
of bringing them together.

00:35:50.320 --> 00:35:54.017
There'll be some folks who are
particularly good at solving the technical

00:35:54.017 --> 00:35:54.551
problem.

00:35:54.551 --> 00:36:00.840
There are one or two folks who their
forte is operations and just executing.

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:01.584
There are one or

00:36:01.584 --> 00:36:04.740
two folks are going to be very good
at the fundraising element of it.

00:36:04.740 --> 00:36:08.622
Constantly thinking about
the cash flow analysis,

00:36:08.622 --> 00:36:11.620
warning that the spend is very high, and

00:36:11.620 --> 00:36:16.839
don't think you have as much runway
as you think you have, all right?

00:36:16.839 --> 00:36:22.710
So I think that all of these talents
really have to come together, and I think

00:36:22.710 --> 00:36:28.886
a founder has to appreciate that it's
only a genius that is good in everything.

00:36:28.886 --> 00:36:35.011
That's why I think the business history,
which all founders worldwide study,

00:36:35.011 --> 00:36:39.163
has to let them know, that yes,
there was Bill Gates.

00:36:39.163 --> 00:36:41.110
Yes, there was Steve Jobs.

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:45.621
But, without a doubt, there were two or
three other people who

00:36:45.621 --> 00:36:50.130
worked with Bill Gates,
who you don't hear as much about, but

00:36:50.130 --> 00:36:55.769
who were indispensable to the overall
success, and the same with Steve Jobs.

00:36:55.769 --> 00:37:00.587
But the weight is one person gets
a disproportionate share of the limelight

00:37:00.587 --> 00:37:05.112
and therefore it creates a misleading
sense of why you're successful

00:37:05.112 --> 00:37:06.498
&gt;&gt; I love the mindset point,

00:37:06.498 --> 00:37:09.711
because so many founders in
the early days are being called on

00:37:09.711 --> 00:37:13.939
to solve problems that are way outside
their comfort zone and they have to do it.

00:37:13.939 --> 00:37:14.590
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:17.584
&gt;&gt; But they also have to
understand that a certain point,

00:37:17.584 --> 00:37:20.134
that's going to slow the company down,
right?

00:37:20.134 --> 00:37:21.656
We call it post-heroic leadership,

00:37:21.656 --> 00:37:24.039
where you don't have to be
the hero of every story, right?

00:37:24.039 --> 00:37:27.405
And for technical challenges for
which there is a solution,

00:37:27.405 --> 00:37:30.181
you just maybe have to spend
some money to find it.

00:37:30.181 --> 00:37:31.400
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:32.900
&gt;&gt; That type of leadership can work.

00:37:32.900 --> 00:37:35.625
But for what we call in the Stanford
program, adaptive challenges.

00:37:35.625 --> 00:37:36.693
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:36.693 --> 00:37:40.739
&gt;&gt; Challenges to your strategy,
competitive landscape,

00:37:40.739 --> 00:37:45.217
exogenous shocks,
that there without a team, you're lost.

00:37:45.217 --> 00:37:47.606
And if the team thinks that you're
going to have all the answers,

00:37:47.606 --> 00:37:49.015
they're not going to come up with any.

00:37:49.015 --> 00:37:52.975
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and
I think it also introduces and

00:37:52.975 --> 00:37:55.650
confronts this issue of trust.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:38:00.523
Trust in the sense that, here you are,
you're in a country in which there

00:38:00.523 --> 00:38:04.626
are different ethnic groups,
different religious groups.

00:38:04.626 --> 00:38:10.248
And you're going to have to work with
people, often that you don't know,

00:38:10.248 --> 00:38:14.099
in a society in which it
is not heavily structured.

00:38:14.099 --> 00:38:18.689
In a society in which there is not
as much rule of law elsewhere.

00:38:18.689 --> 00:38:23.742
And therefore the need to work with
people that you trust becomes essential.

00:38:23.742 --> 00:38:28.253
And oftentimes the problem of
the founder is that he has

00:38:28.253 --> 00:38:31.496
difficulty in trusting other people.

00:38:31.496 --> 00:38:35.184
Because he's heard so
many stories about how A trusted B and

00:38:35.184 --> 00:38:36.968
B took off with the company.

00:38:36.968 --> 00:38:41.398
[MUSIC]

00:38:41.398 --> 00:38:45.484
&gt;&gt; Hakeem's views on leadership reflect
his broader views about the individual and

00:38:45.484 --> 00:38:48.634
the collective, and
what truly makes a business successful.

00:38:48.634 --> 00:38:51.343
[MUSIC]

00:38:51.343 --> 00:38:56.325
&gt;&gt; You were a guest keynote speaker at
the Stanford Africa Business Forum earlier

00:38:56.325 --> 00:38:57.087
this year.

00:38:57.087 --> 00:39:01.965
And you made a comment that business
leaders in Africa are overrated.

00:39:01.965 --> 00:39:06.167
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I really think that,
I think for two reasons.

00:39:06.167 --> 00:39:11.879
One it's because just kind of
businessman in Africa, really have,

00:39:11.879 --> 00:39:18.094
as his ultimate objective, only
the maximization of shareholder value.

00:39:18.094 --> 00:39:21.646
We have the debate in
the United States all the time.

00:39:21.646 --> 00:39:26.360
Part of me thinks in the United States
it's an easier question simply because

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:28.839
this is already a very wealthy society.

00:39:28.839 --> 00:39:32.815
Now, in Africa, the imbalance of power,

00:39:32.815 --> 00:39:36.687
the skewed nature of power is even worse.

00:39:36.687 --> 00:39:41.204
So to me, one of the things that
worries me a lot is that we've

00:39:41.204 --> 00:39:45.635
spoken a lot about how business
should be in the forefront,

00:39:45.635 --> 00:39:49.105
the government should leave and
hand it over.

00:39:49.105 --> 00:39:53.611
But we still haven't found how, for
example, the private sector, which is

00:39:53.611 --> 00:40:00.500
supposed to be heroic, how does it deliver
Low cost medicine to the population.

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:07.164
How can it deliver infrastructure that
goes outside of the capital city and

00:40:07.164 --> 00:40:10.740
of one or two other commercial centers?

00:40:10.740 --> 00:40:13.390
How does it develop?

00:40:13.390 --> 00:40:15.870
Low cost education health everybody.

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:16.591
We haven't.

00:40:16.591 --> 00:40:20.155
&gt;&gt; But I think you've answered the
question you didn't want me to ask also,

00:40:20.155 --> 00:40:20.903
which is that,

00:40:20.903 --> 00:40:24.543
it's not that you have to abandon
the maximization of shareholder value.

00:40:24.543 --> 00:40:27.166
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; It's that your success should matter to

00:40:27.166 --> 00:40:28.730
someone other than you.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:29.320
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:31.992
&gt;&gt; That's when [CROSSTALK] we tried to
pick companies for the Seed Program.

00:40:31.992 --> 00:40:32.551
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:40:32.551 --> 00:40:36.014
&gt;&gt; Of course we like social enterprises
that are commercially viable, but

00:40:36.014 --> 00:40:37.431
the question I always ask is,

00:40:37.431 --> 00:40:41.021
if this company grows 10x will it
matter to anyone other than the owners?

00:40:41.021 --> 00:40:42.328
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:40:42.328 --> 00:40:46.160
&gt;&gt; Are they solving a problem that I care
about, that people there care about?

00:40:46.160 --> 00:40:49.430
Are they solving a meaningful and
important problem?

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:53.584
&gt;&gt; But that problem is even more
acute in Africa and therefore,

00:40:53.584 --> 00:40:55.620
more and more and more.

00:40:55.620 --> 00:41:00.999
We have to measure how well we're doing,
not by how many people

00:41:00.999 --> 00:41:06.378
on the Forbes cover,
not by how many billionaires Africa has,

00:41:06.378 --> 00:41:11.073
but basically what has
happened to the bottom 40%.

00:41:11.073 --> 00:41:15.829
Are we able to say,
as the Chinese can say, with many things

00:41:15.829 --> 00:41:20.776
we don't like about the political system,
but they can say,

00:41:20.776 --> 00:41:26.606
we over the last 40 years have lifted
x hundred million out of poverty?

00:41:26.606 --> 00:41:31.447
And I think that business strategy,
business success has

00:41:31.447 --> 00:41:35.989
got to be a strategy that
yes enables great companies,

00:41:35.989 --> 00:41:39.644
allows people to make
certain effective and

00:41:39.644 --> 00:41:43.712
enterprising people to
make good sums of money.

00:41:43.712 --> 00:41:48.318
But it is also got to be a system
that lifts out of poverty

00:41:48.318 --> 00:41:53.320
large proportions of
the overall population.

00:41:53.320 --> 00:41:56.400
&gt;&gt; I think this is the most
compelling part of Hakeem's outlook.

00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:01.025
That success on both the micro and macro
level is never about just one person,

00:42:01.025 --> 00:42:03.240
it's about everybody.

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:06.931
It's reflected in how he leads his
views on regulators and his hopes for

00:42:06.931 --> 00:42:09.290
the future of the continent.

00:42:09.290 --> 00:42:11.760
It's a vision that we at
Stanford Seed share and

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:14.580
it's why he's such a good fit for us.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:16.321
I'd like to thank Hakeem Belo-Osagie.

00:42:16.321 --> 00:42:20.346
We're lucky to have him on the podcast and
as our board chair.

00:42:20.346 --> 00:42:24.780
Hakeem's philosophy aligns so well with
what we're doing here at Stanford Seed.

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.594
And we're excited to work with him
to make our programs even better.

00:42:27.594 --> 00:42:32.717
